#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9cfff4db-e33e-5a3c-a1d4-99e910f2c2fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-10897" :question/problem "Simplify the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx$ using symmetry." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 2\\int_{0}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x) = x \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nis an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= (-x)\\sin (-x) \\\\\n&=-x(-\\sin x) \\\\\n&= x\\sin x \\\\\n&= f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because\n\n$$\nf(x) = x \\sin{x}\n$$\n\nis an even function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx = 2 \\int_{0}^2 x \\sin{x} \\, \\textrm dx\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "a3e0d847-4dd1-5a47-948c-06959065b73f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10898" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^2 (e^x+e^{-x}) \\, \\textrm dx$\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\pi}^{2\\pi} \\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm dx$" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x) = 4x^3$ is an odd function:\n$f(-x)|= 4(-x)^{3}; =-4x^{3}; =-f(x)✓$\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n$\\int_{-3}^3 4x^3 \\, \\textrm dx = 0$.\n\n- The function $g(x) = e^x+e^{-x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n$g(-x)|= e^{(-x)} + e^{- (-x)}; = e^{-x} + e^{x}; = e^{x} + e^{-x}; \\ne - g(x)\\cdot$\n\n- The function $h(x) = \\sin(2x)$ is an odd function, but integral III is not of the form $\\displaystyle \\int_{-a}^a h(x)\\,\\textrm d x$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. So, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156928 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebbaf66e-6155-510f-b4d8-e38dd4c4f05c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Odd Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "29ca6f83-ceb9-54fa-b8c4-6c0fbbd4b742" :question/math-academy-id "e-10911" :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = A$, what is the value of $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^0 x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x) = f(x)$\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x) = -f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-a}^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0\n$$\n\nNotice that\n\n$$\nf(x) = x\\cos x\n$$\n\nis an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(-x) &= (-x)\\cos (-x) \\\\\n&=-x\\cos x \\\\\n&= - (x\\cos x) \\\\\n&=-f(x)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because\n\n$$\nf(x) = x\\cos x\n$$\n\nis an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-1}^{1} x\\cos x \\, \\textrm dx = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{-1}^{1}x\\cos xdx &= 0 \\\\\n∫_{-1}^{0}x\\cos xdx + ∫_{0}^{1}x\\cos xdx &= 0 \\\\\n∫_{-1}^{0}x\\cos xdx + A &= 0 \\\\\n∫_{-1}^{0}x\\cos xdx &= -A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2688742c-1655-557a-888d-af7e2179a89c" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a32f71bb-d579-5a09-adf4-c4e8b8941ac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156599} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a1db3b-fcf8-51cf-b063-025595438bbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156600} {:answer/id #uuid "0bc869c3-e5e1-5d34-8018-4c693e813254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156601} {:answer/id #uuid "138b1df1-7ce1-518a-a848-5ec59fccc910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156602} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf5e3a4-2f1c-501a-ac3f-2d9d58d963a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "138b1df1-7ce1-518a-a848-5ec59fccc910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2∫_{0}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156602} :answer-field/id #uuid "05e1fb37-faf4-5c32-b77c-b90a3655ca33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156598}] :question/id #uuid "a8d33409-c623-5f70-8121-08ea4427ad09" :question/math-academy-id "q-88206" :question/problem "What is $∫_{-3}^{3}x^{2}\\cos (5x)dx$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a247336-9bc8-5799-9956-e3de188ce2d2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Integrals That Evaluate to Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf4ad931-d6c5-5a3d-914c-c276a009d84c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156611} {:answer/id #uuid "cbaf6f33-be77-5b1a-8c72-98ed6a1a8fb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156612} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc0cd67-7df9-568a-bc9b-0c7d65892443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156613} {:answer/id #uuid "e35981b8-f69e-5e88-946e-767277ca3b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156614} {:answer/id #uuid "17d4dea2-d5ec-5f4c-8bfc-1c46ee872135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d4dea2-d5ec-5f4c-8bfc-1c46ee872135" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156615} :answer-field/id #uuid "799ba412-3bb6-5597-813b-29c7be335d75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156610}] :question/id #uuid "78e1f8f9-d432-5676-a7be-df1e563ec326" :question/math-academy-id "q-88209" :question/problem "Which of the following integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry?\n\n1. $∫_{-2}^{2}3x^{2}dx$\n2. $∫_{-3}^{6}5x^{3}dx$\n3. $∫_{-9}^{9}2x^{5}dx$"}]}
